      "latency_ms": 3008.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 584,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**. \n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because, under normal Earth conditions, it naturally exists in three distinct forms:\n  * **Liquid:** The standard form we drink and swim in.\n  * **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which expands and becomes less dense than liquid water (which is why ice floats).\n  * **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water gas in the air or boiling steam.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater has several unusual and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it transports vital nutrients in our bodies and minerals through the soil.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\n* **Earth’s Covering:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth in a closed system known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes, forms clouds in the atmosphere, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into bodies of water.\n\n### 4. The Biological View (Life)\n* **The Medium of Life:** Life as we know it originated in water, and all living things require it. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature through perspiration, and flushes out waste. Without water, human cells cannot function, and dehydration leads to death within days.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes our planet's geography, drives our weather and climate, and serves as the literal foundation of biology and life."
